<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP, permitted for exactly ONE host.

  Why cleartext is needed at all: the transcription service lives on a Nebula
  mesh at 10.42.0.10 and speaks plain HTTP. The transport is not actually
  unprotected — every byte is already inside an authenticated, encrypted
  Nebula tunnel, and the service has no TLS listener and no certificate a
  phone could validate. Terminating TLS inside the tunnel would mean issuing
  and distributing a private CA to the handset for no confidentiality we do
  not already have.

  🔴 Why this is scoped to a single domain instead of
  android:usesCleartextTraffic="true":

  The blanket attribute disables cleartext policy for EVERY destination the
  app will ever talk to, which is a real security downgrade and a permanent
  one — it would silently permit an unencrypted request to any host, on any
  network, including a hostile Wi-Fi. The mesh address is a fixed, private,
  non-routable address with a tunnel underneath it; "that one host" and "the
  whole internet" are not the same claim, and only the first one is true.

  cleartextTrafficPermitted is false everywhere else by inheriting the
  platform default for targetSdk 28+, which is what base-config restates
  explicitly below so that a future edit cannot widen it by accident.

  includeSubdomains is absent on purpose: 10.42.0.10 is a literal address and
  has no subdomains, so including them would only broaden the rule's reach
  if the entry were later changed to a hostname.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <!-- Everything not named below: cleartext refused. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <!-- The self-hosted ASR service, reachable only over the mesh. -->
        <domain>10.42.0.10</domain>
    </domain-config>

</network-security-config>
